Measuring a PSF from beads
Image sub-resolution fluorescent beads (100 nm or smaller), then:

subtract the background (camera offset, autofluorescence);
find each bead’s centre with sub-pixel accuracy (e.g. its centroid);
shift every bead to a common centre and average many of them;
normalise to sum 1. Report the FWHM.

What limits it:

Bead size: the image is PSF ∗ bead. A bead of diameter D widens the measured PSF roughly like an extra blur of FWHM 0.53D: \text{FWHM}_\text{meas}^2 \approx \text{FWHM}_\text{PSF}^2 + (0.53D)^2.
Noise: averaging N beads reduces it by \sqrt N. Background left in widens the PSF. Pixels coarser than Nyquist blur the average.
Measured vs computed: the measured PSF contains the real aberrations of your microscope (often asymmetric along z); a computed one is noise-free but idealised.

Other machines that measure line integrals
The rest of the day only needs “each measurement = a line integral”. The same mathematics serves

electron tomography: a thin specimen tilted in the microscope; each image is a projection of the specimen’s density;
PET and SPECT: a radioactive tracer emits photons; detectors count the emissions along lines (no logarithm: the counts themselves are the sums);
optical projection tomography: cleared embryos or organs imaged with visible light from many angles.

Volume rendering (the 3D graphics elective) computes projections of a known volume to look at it; tomography does the opposite: it has the projections and wants the volume.

The forward model in one line
\text{image} = \text{sample}\big(\text{object} \ast \text{PSF}\big) + \text{noise}
It assumes the PSF is the same everywhere (shift invariance). That fails when:

the lens is worse at the edge of the field (aberrations grow off-axis);
we image deep into thick tissue: scattering and a refractive-index mismatch (oil objective, watery sample) widen and skew the PSF with depth;
the sample moves during the exposure in some places only.

Then the image is a sum of copies of different PSFs: no longer one convolution. Common remedy: treat small patches separately.

The math: the stack as formulas
With L_\sigma = G_\sigma * I the image smoothed at scale \sigma:

intensity L_\sigma; edges \|\nabla L_\sigma\| = \sqrt{L_x^2 + L_y^2}; blobs \sigma^2 \nabla^2 L_\sigma = \sigma^2(L_{xx} + L_{yy});
ridges: the eigenvalues \lambda_1 \ge \lambda_2 of the Hessian \sigma^2\begin{pmatrix}L_{xx} & L_{xy}\\ L_{xy} & L_{yy}\end{pmatrix};
texture: the eigenvalues \mu_1 \ge \mu_2 of the structure tensor G_{\sigma/2} * \big(\nabla L_\sigma \nabla L_\sigma^{\top}\big), times \sigma^2.

The factor \sigma^2 makes responses comparable across scales. Derived and explained on the edges, blobs and ridges day; here they are only inputs.

Pose normalisation instead of invariants
Bring every shape into a standard pose, then compare anything (moments, pixels):

translate the centroid to the origin;
scale to area 1;
rotate the major axis (the labelling day’s orientation) onto x;
fix the remaining 180° ambiguity by the sign of \mu_{30} (heavier side to the left, say).

Fails when \lambda_1 \approx \lambda_2 (square, star, gear: no major axis) or \mu_{30} \approx 0. For a shape with k-fold symmetry, use the phase of c_{k0} instead: it turns by k\alpha.
The snakes elective aligns shapes by Procrustes, which needs corresponding landmarks; pose normalisation needs none, at the price of these failure cases.

What k-means assumes
Every boundary is a straight bisector between two centres, and every point belongs to exactly one group. So k-means works best for groups that are

round (same spread in every direction): an elongated group is cut across, because cutting a long group in half lowers J more than separating two parallel groups;
of similar size: the boundary sits halfway between the centres, so a big, spread-out group loses its outer points to a small neighbour;
measured in comparable units: distances add squared differences of all features. Rescale one axis (µm instead of mm, or intensity × 10) and the groups change.

The next units replace the round, equal groups by Gaussians with their own shape, size and weight.

Set operations for free
With \phi_1 and \phi_2 for two shapes (negative inside):

union (inside either): \min(\phi_1, \phi_2);
intersection (inside both): \max(\phi_1, \phi_2);
complement (swap inside and outside): -\phi;
difference A \setminus B: \max(\phi_1, -\phi_2).

Two circles of radius 1 centred at (\pm 1.2, 0): \phi = \min\big(\sqrt{(x - 1.2)^2 + y^2} - 1,\ \sqrt{(x + 1.2)^2 + y^2} - 1\big). At the origin \phi = 0.2: outside both, in the gap between them.

Recall: two Gaussians and a threshold
The thresholding day modelled the histogram as two Gaussians (object and background) and put the threshold where the two weighted densities cross: the minimum-error threshold (Kittler–Illingworth). It found the two Gaussians by trying every threshold t and fitting each side separately.
Today, the same model:

with K classes instead of two;
fitted by EM: soft assignments instead of a hard split at t;
giving every pixel a probability of each class, not only a label.

By the end of this exercise you should be able to:

read a 2D Gaussian from its picture: the mean, the covariance ellipse with the eigenvectors of \Sigma as axes and \sqrt{\lambda_i} as half-lengths;
compute and interpret the Mahalanobis distance;
count the parameters of a mixture for each covariance type;
describe a mixture as a recipe for data, and compute responsibilities by Bayes’ rule;
(math) derive the maximum-likelihood mean of one Gaussian and the linear boundary of tied covariances.

