The math: orthonormal transforms and Parseval
An orthonormal transform c = A x with A^\top A = I (rows = basis vectors):

inverse x = A^\top c: the image is a sum of basis images, weighted by the coefficients;
Parseval: \|c\|^2 = c^\top c = x^\top A^\top A x = \|x\|^2;
dropping or changing coefficients by \delta c changes the image by \delta x = A^\top \delta c with \|\delta x\| = \|\delta c\|: the error of a quantised coefficient goes unchanged into the image;
keeping the coefficients k \in K: squared error = \sum_{k \notin K} c_k^2. Best K for one block: the largest |c_k|.
2 points: A = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}, a rotation by 45° (and a reflection).

Local mean and local standard deviation
Two tables: one of I, one of I^2. For any window, four lookups each give

the local mean m = (sum of I) / (number of pixels): a blur;
the mean of the squares \overline{I^2} = (sum of I^2) / (number of pixels);
the local variance s^2 = \overline{I^2} - m^2 and the local standard deviation s.

s is small in flat regions and large at edges and in textured regions: a simple texture feature for every pixel. (Its counterpart for whole segmented objects is on the measurements day.)

The Bland–Altman plot
For each image: the difference (auto − manual) against the mean of the two.

Manual 20, 35, 50, 65, 80, 100; auto 19, 33, 46, 60, 72, 88. r = 0.9995, yet:

bias = mean difference = −5.33 (a systematic undercount);
limits of agreement = bias ± 1.96 SD = −13.3 to +2.7: the range of the error for a new image;
the cloud slopes down: the undercount grows with the count (proportional bias, e.g. more merges in dense images). Then the limits mislead; compare ratios (or work on a log scale): auto/manual drops from 0.95 to 0.88.

Collapse: infinite likelihood
Put one component exactly on one data point and let its variance shrink:

its density at that point is 1/\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^2} \to \infty, so \log p(\mathcal D) \to +\infty;
the other points are still explained by the other components, so nothing stops it.

The “best” fit is then nonsense: a spike on one pixel value. EM can run into this, especially with many components, small groups or outliers.
Remedies:

a variance floor: add \varepsilon I to every \Sigma_k after the M-step;
restart a component that collapses (or that becomes empty, N_k \to 0);
a prior on \Sigma_k (Bayesian mixtures; a pointer only).

Recall: covariance and principal axes
The PCA unit (day 1) showed that the eigenvectors of a covariance matrix are the principal axes of a point cloud, and each eigenvalue \lambda_i is the variance along its axis. The labelling day’s “equivalent ellipse” of an object is the same picture.
For a Gaussian:

the ellipse axes point along the eigenvectors \mathbf v_1, \mathbf v_2 of \Sigma;
the half-lengths of the 1σ ellipse are \sqrt{\lambda_1} and \sqrt{\lambda_2} (the 2σ ellipse is twice as large);
the off-diagonal entry tilts the ellipse; with c = 0 the axes are the coordinate axes.

