The two-pass algorithm

Rosenfeld & Pfaltz (1966). First pass, in raster order, at each object pixel look only at the neighbours already visited (8: up-left, up, up-right, left; 4: up, left):

none labelled → a new provisional label;
one label → copy it;
several labels → copy the smallest and record that they are equivalent.

Then resolve the equivalences into classes, number them 1, 2, 3, …, and in a second pass replace each provisional label by its class number.

Union–find
The equivalences form sets that keep merging: \{1, 2\}, then \{1, 2, 5\}, … A disjoint-set forest stores them:

each label points to a parent; a set is a tree, named by its root (parent[r] = r);
find(x): follow the parents to the root;
union(a, b): find both roots and hang one under the other.


Naively the trees can become chains (left): find costs up to n steps. Two fixes: union by rank (hang the shallower tree under the deeper one) and path compression (on the way up, point every visited node at the root).

Live-wire: an edge-following cost
Intelligent scissors (Mortensen & Barrett, 1995; Falcão et al., 1998): the user clicks on a boundary, and the cheapest path follows the edges. The local cost of entering a pixel is low on edges:

gradient magnitude: low where the gradient is strong, 1 - G/G_{\max};
Laplacian zero crossing: 0 on the zero crossing of the Laplacian of Gaussian (the centre of the edge), 1 elsewhere;
gradient direction: a penalty for running across the edge direction instead of along it;

each with a weight, after Gaussian smoothing with scale \sigma (the edges day owns these filters). For a thin bright or dark line, the line response itself.

Matching: one to one, at a threshold
Choose an IoU threshold \tau (often 0.5). A true object and a predicted object may be matched only if their IoU \ge \tau, and every object takes at most one partner.

matched pair: a true positive (TP);
predicted object without partner: a false positive (FP);
true object without partner: a false negative (FN).

There are no true-negative objects: the background is not made of objects. So no “accuracy” and no ROC curve for objects.
